from review import card,save,DATE
i='TCS-4417';_,c=card(i);refs=c['references'];refs[0]['locator']='Introduction and Section 1.1 define directed BP/BST and the comparison model; Theorem 3, Section 4 reductions, Section 5 word-RAM result, and first question in Section 7.'
refs += [dict(id='correction',title='Bottleneck Paths Reduce to Deterministic Graphical Games and a Counterexample to a Claimed Linear-Time Algorithm',authors='Egor Gorbachev',year=2026,url='https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.04279',pdf_url='https://arxiv.org/pdf/2608.04279v2',locator='Preprint v2, 6 August 2026, first submitted 4 August. Introduction retains the comparison-based BP question; Section 2 corrects the claimed DGG algorithm in the 2016 paper, and Theorem 3.1/Corollary 3.2 give the BP-to-DGG reduction. Title follows the v2 PDF.')]
save(i,dict(title='Linear-time directed bottleneck paths and trees',formal=r'Do the directed bottleneck path and rooted bottleneck spanning-tree problems admit comparison-based algorithms with expected running time \(O(n+m)\) on every input, always returning an exact optimum? The input has \(n\) vertices and \(m\) weighted directed edges. Randomization is allowed; deterministic linear time would also answer positively. The source writes \(O(m)\) after the usual removal or treatment of irrelevant vertices. The requested guarantee is total running time, including graph processing and output, with weights accessible only through comparisons.',definitions='''In the bottleneck path problem (BP), the input is an explicit directed graph G=(V,E), a source s, a target t and a weight w(e) for every edge. Among all directed s-to-t paths P, output one minimizing max_{e∈P}w(e). Any optimal simple path is acceptable; ties require no prescribed choice. If t is unreachable, report that no path exists. When s=t the empty path is returned, with bottleneck conventionally −∞.

In the bottleneck spanning-tree problem (BST), the input is the same kind of directed weighted graph and a specified root s. Output an out-arborescence T spanning all vertices: s has indegree zero, every other vertex has indegree one in T, and there is a directed path in T from s to every vertex. Minimize max_{e∈T}w(e). If no such tree exists, report this. For a one-vertex graph, the empty tree has value −∞. This objective minimizes the single largest tree-edge weight, not the sum of weights. A tree minimizing that largest weight need not supply an individually optimal bottleneck path to every vertex.

Weights are arbitrary elements of a totally ordered set, such as arbitrary real numbers. They are opaque keys: they may be stored, copied and compared in constant time, but their digits, arithmetic values and integer ranks are not available for computation. Equal weights are allowed. The graph is given by adjacency lists with O(log(n+m+2))-bit vertex and edge indices; ordinary operations on these indices have unit cost. The algorithm is charged for all graph operations, comparisons, random sampling and writing its path or tree, not just the number of comparisons. There is no assumption that the weights are sorted, bounded integers, or machine words supporting bit operations.

The randomization convention is Las Vegas: on every fixed input the answer is exact, termination occurs with probability one, and the expected total work over the algorithm's internal random choices is at most C(n+m) for a universal constant C. This includes feasibility checks and output. A randomized algorithm with a small probability of an incorrect optimum would not meet this convention. For BP, isolated and unreachable vertices can be removed in O(n+m) work. A feasible nontrivial BST instance has m≥n−1. Thus O(n+m) makes the source's O(m) target meaningful also on degenerate explicit inputs without changing its substantive content.

The question asks for this guarantee for both tasks, but their expected-linear-time versions are equivalent through the reductions in Section 4 of the source. These reductions do not establish the corresponding deterministic equivalence: the BST-to-BP reduction uses randomness. The source's separate question about a deterministic O(mβ(m,n)) bound is not the target of this card. Neither all-pairs bottleneck paths nor finding every individual single-source bottleneck value is required.''',question_type='yes_no',answer_criterion='Give always-correct comparison-based algorithms with expected O(n+m) total work for both directed tasks, or use the source reductions to derive both guarantees from one. Alternatively, rule out such algorithms, stating any assumptions used. An undirected algorithm, a word-RAM algorithm inspecting weight representations, a bound on comparisons alone, or a faster algorithm only for sufficiently dense graphs does not settle the unrestricted target. A deterministic lower bound alone does not rule out the permitted randomized algorithms.',context_blocks=[dict(title='A very small overhead remains',text='Theorem 3 gives expected O(mβ(m,n)) time, where β(m,n) is the least k≥1 for which the k-times iterated base-two logarithm of n is at most m/n, in the nondegenerate regime m≥n. This overhead grows very slowly, and the theorem is already linear when m≥n log^(k)n for any fixed k. The question includes sparse graphs where β is unbounded.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The computational model is decisive',text='The source already obtains deterministic linear time in the word-RAM model and recalls linear-time algorithms for undirected graphs. Its open question concerns arbitrary directed graphs with opaque comparison-only weights.',citation='primary'),dict(title='Paths and trees are related, but not identical outputs',text='Section 4 relates the two problems at the expected-linear scale. The spanning-tree objective is one global bottleneck; it should not be replaced with the stronger requirement to return every single-source optimal bottleneck path.',citation='primary'),dict(title='A 2026 correction concerns the related games result',text='Gorbachev’s August 2026 preprint gives a counterexample to the 2016 paper’s claimed linear-time algorithm for deterministic graphical games and a linear reduction from directed BP to the designated-start game-value problem. It explicitly retains the BP question. This correction concerns the DGG claim, not the paper’s stated randomized BP/BST upper bound or its word-RAM result.',citation='correction')],why='Determines the exact cost of fundamental directed min-max optimization when numerical information is available only by comparison. Removing the overhead would close a long-standing gap for sparse graphs; distinguishing the input model prevents an already-solved variant from being presented as open.',progress=[dict(date='2016',text='Theorem 3 improves the comparison-based expected time to O(mβ(m,n)); Section 5 separately gives deterministic linear time when word-RAM operations on weights are allowed.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2026-08-06',text='The v2 preprint identifies a flaw in the claimed DGG algorithm and reduces directed BP to DGG value computation in linear time. It still states linear-time comparison-based BP as open.',citation='correction')],references=refs,status='open',status_note='Checked the original definitions, Theorem 3, both reductions, the word-RAM result and the exact Section 7 questions, plus the August 2026 v2 preprint including its counterexample and reduction statement. The main target permits always-correct randomization, matching the preceding expected-time theorem; the separate deterministic near-linear question is not silently substituted. Searches through 12 September 2026 found no resolution. The recent correction is a preprint and its proofs were not independently audited.',model_self_contained=True,requires_context=False,review_note='Defined the two directed optimization outputs, opaque-weight comparison model, expected total work and degenerate cases. Removed the suggested threshold-connectivity strategy. Added the August 2026 correction with its precise DGG scope.',importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='An exact-complexity question for basic directed bottleneck optimization, separating comparison access from more powerful numerical models. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['A bottleneck path minimizes its heaviest edge, and a rooted bottleneck tree minimizes the heaviest edge needed to reach all vertices.','The open target is expected linear total time for exact solutions on arbitrary directed graphs using only comparisons of edge weights.','The source already achieves a very slowly growing overhead and gives linear time in the stronger word-RAM model.','The path and tree targets are equivalent at the expected-linear scale through the source reductions.','An August 2026 preprint corrects a related graphical-games claim and continues to state the comparison-based path question as open.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','correction'])),notes=['Recovered the directed BP and out-arborescence objectives and the comparison-only model.','Specified always-correct expected linear total work, output costs and infeasible or degenerate inputs.','Separated the solved word-RAM/undirected variants and the stronger all-single-source output.','Removed the proposed solution route and incorporated the August 2026 DGG correction without misattributing it to the BP/BST theorem.'],checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs])
